# Three small NPS studies, text from typescripts (OCR noise in places; quotes typed in clean spelling and matched letter for letter):
#  - The Significance of the Harmon Farm and the Springs Hotel Woods (park historian; deeds, claims, Adams Sentinel, accounts of Amelia Harmon)
#  - Vista Cutting Project: Area of Little Round Top, Devil's Den, the Wheatfield and Peach Orchard (park report of about 1939-40)
#  - Proposed Restoration of the Spangler's Spring-Culp's Hill Area and the Re-Location of Avenues (park report of 1940)
# The 1939-40 reports describe the ground as it was then; they are marked with their own date, and the earthwork heights are 1930s measurements of works that the
# park commission had resodded and rebuilt, not 1863 profiles.
@ NPS-HARMON-FARM
%basis deeds (Adams County Deed Books), the Adams Sentinel, damage claims and accounts of Amelia Harmon as cited in the study
5 | woodlots | Harmon/Wible Woods: a 43-acre tract once divided among three farms, with worm fences and stone walls | - | pre-1863 (three farms; worm fences and stone walls) | stated | The woods later held by the Springs Hotel Company, a 43-acre tract, had been divided among three separate farms, and their interior boundaries disappeared as the historic worm fences decayed and were burned and the stone walls taken apart | =The ownership of the woods by the Springs and Hotel Company had dissolved all previous boundaries, which had at one time divided the 43-acre tract amongst three separate farms. Because the company had purchased all three of those farms during different stages of its development, only the exterior boundaries of the woods were retained. Interior boundaries, visual and physical, eventually disappeared as the historic worm fences decayed and were burned, and as stone walls were disassembled for other purposes.
7 | buildings | Harman farm 1841: pump and cistern, brick wash and smoke house, 80 by 54 foot stone bank barn | - | 1841 (advertisement) | stated | The 1841 description lists a never-failing pump and cistern, a two-story brick wash and smoke house and an 80-foot by 54-foot double stone bank barn with a wagon shed and a corn crib | =At the back door of the brick house was a “never failing pump and cistern” and a two-story brick wash and smoke house. Nearby were all the “usual outbuildings,” including an 80-foot by 54-foot double stone bank barn, with a wagon shed and a corn crib.
7 | orchards | Harman farm orchard 1841: about 160-170 apple trees with peach, cherry and plum | - | 1841 (advertisement) | stated | An orchard of prime engrafted apple trees, about 160-170 in number, surrounded the buildings, with peach, cherry and plum trees adding variety to the mostly apple orchard | =An orchard of “prime engrafted apple trees, about 160-170 in number” also surrounded the buildings, and peach, cherry, and plum trees gave variety to the predominantly apple orchard.
7 | woodlots | Woodlands of the larger Harman farm lay north of Mill Road | - | pre-1863 | stated | All woodlands and woodlots of the larger farm were north of Mill Road and none passed with the open tenant farm | =All of the woodlands/woodlots associated with the larger farm were north of Mill Road, and none of those were transferred with the open tenant farm.
10 | buildings | Harman farm 1862: twelve-room brick house, brick wash house, smoke house, stone bank barn | - | 1862 (advertisement) | stated | The 1862 sale description gives a large twelve-room brick dwelling with kitchen, a two-story brick wash house, a smoke house and a stone bank barn with attached wagon shed and corn crib | =The farmhouse was described as a large twelve-room brick dwelling with a kitchen inside. There was also the two-story brick wash house, the smoke house, and a stone bank barn with attached wagon shed and corn crib.
10 | damage | Harman farm laid waste, one of a handful of battlefield farms burned for military reasons | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Harman farm was laid waste, one of only a handful of battlefield farms burned for military reasons, and its woods were splintered by shot and shell | Before the armies departed to renew their bloody grappling in Virginia >>> shot and shell
16 | fields | Large timothy field between the Harman barn and the woods (Amelia Harmon's account) | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | Amelia Harmon later described a large timothy field between the barn and the woods hiding hundreds of gray figures advancing under its cover | =This one glance showed us that a large timothy field between the barn and the woods concealed hundreds of gray crouching figures stealthily advancing under its cover, and picking off every cavalry man who appeared for an instance in sight.
18 | woodlots | Thick undergrowth along Willoughby Run in front of Archer | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | General Archer's front was covered by thick undergrowth along Willoughby Run while Davis had a clear field of view unhindered by woods | =Although General Davis had a clear field of vision to his immediate front, unhindered by woods, General Archer’s front was covered by thick undergrowth along Willoughby’s Run.
34 | fences | Frederick Herr claim: 7,500 chestnut rails, 3,000 feet of fencing boards | - | 1863-07 | stated | Frederick Herr, who owned land just west of the Harmon/Wible Woods, claimed 7,500 chestnut rails, 3,000 feet of fencing boards, 75 posts and two cords of wood, mostly burned for cookfires or broken down to make way for troops and wagons | =Frederick Herr, who owned property immediately west of the Harmon/Wible Woods, claimed the loss of 7500 chestnut rails, 3000 feet of fencing boards, 75 posts, and two cords of wood. Most of these wood products were removed and burned for cookfires, or were broken down to make way for large bodies of men and wagons.
34 | woodlots | Heavy oak timber, 25 acres, southern extension of the Harmon/Wible Woods | - | ~1866 advertisement (post-battle description of a woodlot) | stated | An 1866 advertisement of a 138-acre farm describes a stand of heavy oak timber of 25 acres on its east boundary, the southern extension of the Harmon/Wible Woods | =Included in a description of the 138-acre farm was the stand of “heavy oak timber” which comprised the 25 acres of its eastern boundary {{IAdams|[Adams}} Sentinel (1 September 1866) {{J|]}}. This oak timber was the southern extension of the Harmon/Wible Woods
35 | buildings | Harman brick house said to have survived the fire; Elliott map shows barn and outbuildings burned | - | 1863-07 | conflict | The study says the Elliott map (surveyed August-November 1863) shows the barn and outbuildings burned but not the house, so the brick house seems to have survived, a 1870s photograph showing a brick house with an orchard but no outbuildings or barn; the First Day report instead says the barn and brick house were burned | =That the brick house itself seems to have withstood total devastation by the flames is affirmed by the map of the battlefield by S. G. Elliott, surveyed sometime between August and November of 1863. Elliott’s map indicated that the barn and outbuildings were burned and destroyed during the battle, but the house has no such indication. A photograph taken in the 1870s shows a two-story brick house at the same site as that indicated by a house on the 1868-1869 G. K. Warren survey. This brick house was surrounded by an orchard, but lacked all other outbuildings or barn.

@ NPS-VISTA-CUTTING
%basis the Warren survey map of 1868-69 and Brady and Tipton photographs, as the report names them
9 | woodlots | Sources for the woodland of July 1863: the Warren map and Brady and Tipton photographs | R12.015 | 1939-1940 report on sources | stated | The report names the Warren survey map and available Brady and Tipton photographs as the chief sources for restoring woodland conditions as of July 1863, calling the Warren map, surveyed October 1868 to October 1869, the most authentic | =In establishing a historical basis for vista cutting, which would also involve a restoration of woodland conditions as of July, 1863, the chief sources of information are the Warren Survey Map of the Battlefield of Gettysburg, and available Brady and Tipton photographs. It is felt that the Warren Map, produced from an actual survey of the Battlefield area undertaken from October 21, 1868 to October 23, 1869, under the direction of Major General G. K. Warren, is the most authentic source of information.
10 | woodlots | Western slope of Little Round Top cut clear of woods a year before the battle | - | 1862 | stated | A year before the battle the entire western slope of Little Round Top had been cut clear of woods except for a few scattered trees and light undergrowth | =A year prior to the Battle of Gettysburg, the entire western slope of Little Round Top had been cut clear of woods, with the exception of a few scattered trees and light undergrowth.
10 | woodlots | Tipton photographs are undated and of little use for the 1863 woodland | - | 1939-1940 caution about photographs | stated | The report says the Tipton photographs are undated, so their period cannot be estimated and they are of little use in restoring the woodland conditions of 1863 | =As these Tipton photographs are undated, it is impossible to estimate the approximate period which they represent. These photographs are of little use, therefore, in the effort to restore woodland conditions of the period of the Battle of Gettysburg.
11 | woodlots | Warren map vegetation of Big Round Top and Little Round Top; Brady photographs show the west slope nearly bare | - | 1868-1869 map; 1863 post-battle photographs | stated | On the Warren map Big Round Top shows a heavy growth of round-leaf trees, oak and pine and the western slopes of Little Round Top a considerable growth of pine and oak, while Brady photographs of the post-battle period show the Little Round Top slope nearly devoid of tree growth | =Southward, on the slopes of Big Round Top, a heavy growth of round leaf, oak and pine is observed. Scattered along the western slopes of Little Round Top is a considerable growth of pine and oak. Brady photographs of the post-battle period indicate this slope to have been nearly devoid of tree growth.

@ NPS-SPANGLERS-SPRING
%basis Brady and Tipton photographs and the Warren survey map of 1868-69, as the report names them
15 | woodlots | Spangler's Spring area in 1940: meadow south and east, Culp's Hill heavily timbered | - | 1940 report (condition as then) | stated | The 1940 report describes meadow land south and east of Spangler's Spring and the Culp's Hill area as heavily timbered | =South and east of the Spring is an area of meadow land. The Culp’s Hill area is heavily timbered.
15 | woodlots | Restoration to the timber growth shown on the Warren map of 1868-9 | R12.015 | 1940 plan (restoration to the 1868-69 map) | stated | The plan was to re-establish the timber growth in the Spangler's Spring area as indicated on the Warren survey map of 1868-9 and to replant the area in grass | =This would mean the re-establishment of the timber growth in the area as indicated on the Warren Survey Map of 1868-9, and also the re-planting of the entire Spangler’s Spring area in grass.
15 | earthworks | Stone wall north of the spring used as breastworks by the Confederates; Union works built July 1-2 | E-02,E-03,E-05 | 1863-07-02 | stated | On a ridge just north of the spring a stone wall was used as breastworks by the Confederates on the night of July 2 and the morning of July 3, and the Union earthworks there were hastily built on the night of July 1 and during July 2 | =On a ridge just north of the Spring is a stone wall which was used as breastworks by the Confederates on the night of July 2, and during the Union counter attack on the morning of July 3. The earthworks in this area were hastily constructed by Union troops on the night of July 1 and during July 2.
15 | earthworks | Spangler's Spring to Culp's Hill works: earth, loose stones and logs, about 20 inches (1940 measure) | E-02,E-03,E-05 | 1940 measurement of surviving works (photographs of 1863-1890 cited) | stated | According to Brady and Tipton photographs the works from Spangler's Spring to Culp's Hill were largely earth, loose stones and logs, built to an average height of 20 inches; this is a 1940 description of works the park had restored | =According to Brady and Tipton photographs, the earthworks along the battle line extending from Spangler’s Spring to Culp’s Hill were largely constructed of earth, loose stones and logs and were built up to an average height of 20 inches.
16 | earthworks | Culp's Hill works near Stevens' Knoll 30 inches, 100 yards west of Culp's Hill 26 inches (1940 measure) | E-02,E-05 | 1940 measurement of surviving works | stated | The 1940 report measured a section about 200 yards west of Culp's Hill near Stevens' Knoll at 30 inches and a section 100 yards west of Culp's Hill at an average of 26 inches | =yards west of Culp’s Hill, near Stevens’ Knoll, measuring 30” in height. A section extending 100 yards-westward from Culp’s Hill averages 26” in height.
16 | earthworks | Lunettes on East Cemetery Hill and Stevens' Knoll: earth, 30 inches at highest | E-12 | 1940 measurement of surviving works | stated | In 1940 the lunettes on East Cemetery Hill and Stevens' Knoll, apparently built wholly of earth for the artillery, measured 30 inches at their greatest height and were in good condition; no lunettes were found on Culp's Hill | =The lunettes on East Cemetery Hill and Stevens’ Knoll apparently were constructed entirely of earth and were erected as a protection for the artillery positions. There are no indications of lunettes on Culp’s Hill where Federal batteries had been placed.
